Episodic mild flatulence and subjective abdominal bloating relived spontaneously by defecation
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Postprandial bloating, Frequent flatus, Symptom relief with defecation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-004
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of abdominal discomfort, bloating, and crampy pain requiring assessment to exclude bowel obstruction or acute abdomen.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified crampy abdominal discomfort and subjective increased abdominal girth. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to palpate the abdomen, assess bowel sounds, and rule out surgical emergencies such as early bowel obstruction or appendicitis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My stomach feels tight and bloated, and I have crampy pains after I eat.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a subjective sensation of increased abdominal girth occurring postprandially. Reports frequent passage of flatus throughout the day and intermittent mild crampy abdominal discomfort. Notes immediate relief of pressure following defecation or passing gas. Absence of unintended weight loss. Normal stool consistency without gross blood or melena. Lack of nocturnal awakenings due to gastrointestinal symptoms.
Functional Status: Fully ambulatory, diet maintained.
Social/Safety Concerns: Discomfort is causing mild anxiety regarding potential serious bowel pathology.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/76 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, appears comfortable, no signs of systemic toxicity or writhing pain.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a soft, non-distended abdomen on clinical examination. Normal active bowel sounds present in all four quadrants. Diagnostic triad noted: postprandial bloating, frequent flatus, symptom relief with defecation. No guarding, rigidity, or rebound tenderness. Renal punch negative.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of recent high-FODMAP dietary intake in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No recent antibiotic use or chronic opiate consumption.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Episodic mild flatulence and subjective abdominal bloating relived spontaneously by defecation - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal active bowel sounds, passing of flatus, and soft non-distended abdomen.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of localized right lower quadrant tenderness, no guarding, and normal temperature.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal stool consistency without gross blood or melena, lack of nocturnal awakenings.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute abdominal crampy pain and bloating, symptoms that share a common initial presentation with early mechanical bowel obstruction or visceral perforation. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to perform a thorough abdominal examination. Excluding peritoneal signs and confirming active bowel motility prevented unnecessary escalation to the emergency department while ensuring the patient's condition would not critically deteriorate before regular clinic hours.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance regarding benign nature of current symptoms. Recommendation for over-the-counter simethicone for symptomatic relief. Routine complete blood count and inflammatory markers to be ordered non-urgently by regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep a food and symptom diary for one week to identify dietary triggers, allowing for targeted elimination of offending foods.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss diary findings and blood test results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the abdominal pain becomes severe and constant, if vomiting prevents keeping fluids down, or if blood appears in the stool.

